%[text] %[text:anchor:T_3AA44D56] # Lab 1: Fourier Series
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](file:../../MainMenu.m) 
%%
%[text] **1.** Consider the Fourier sine series:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(t) = \\sum\_{n=1}^\\infty b\_n \\sin \\left(  2 \\pi    t \\right)${"altText":"f(t) = \sum\_{n=1}^\infty b\_n \sin \left(  2 \pi    t \right)"}
%[text] Open the [Sine and Cosine Series app](<matlab: SinCosSeries>). Set the basis to **sine**. Adjust the coefficients to determine the values of $b\_2${"altText":"b\_2"} and $b\_4${"altText":"b\_4"} that generate the function $f${"altText":"f"} pictured below. Round to the nearest 0.5 and record your answer below. The other coefficients are zero.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:46e2)
% Replace the NaNs with your answers
Q1_b2 = 1;
Q1_b4 = -1;
%%
%[text] **2.** Consider the phase-shifted cosine:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $g(t) = 1.4\\cos(4 \\pi t  - \\pi/4 )${"altText":"g(t) = 1.4\cos(4 \pi t  - \pi\/4 )"}
%[text] Use the [Magnitude and Phase app](<matlab: run MagnitudePhase.mlapp>) to recreate the function using Fourier series.
%[text] **a.** Use the magnitude-phase definition (right pane). What are the values of $A\_2${"altText":"A\_2"} and $\\phi\_2${"altText":"\phi\_2"} that recreate the function?
Q2_A2 = NaN;
Q2_phi2 = NaN;
%[text] **b.** Use the sine-cosine definition (left pane). What are the values of $a\_2${"altText":"a\_2"} and $b\_2${"altText":"b\_2"} that recreate the function? Round to the nearest tenth.
Q2_a2 = NaN;
Q2_b2 = NaN;
%%
%[text] **Task.** Q3-5 use data from an audio signal. Run this section to load the data and compute its Fourier series coefficients.
[y,Fs] = audioread("SynthesizedSound.mp4"); % Read the data
sound(y,Fs) % Play the sound
plot(y) % Plot the data
ylabel("y")
[freq,a,b] = computeFourierCoef(y',Fs); % Compute series coef.
%%
%[text] **3.** Compute the magnitude and phase of the Fourier coefficients, which are stored in vectors `a` and `b`. Store the results in `A` and `phi`.
%[text] **Hint:** Use [`atan2`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.atan2.html) to compute the phase.
A = NaN;
phi = NaN;
%%
%[text] **4.** Plot the magnitude vector `A` against the frequency vector `freq`, which is given in units of Hz. Set the horizontal axis limits to `[0 200]` Hz. Also, label the axes.
% Write your code here
plot(freq,A)
xlim([0 200])
xlabel("Frequency (Hz)")
ylabel("Magnitude")
%%
%[text] **5.** There are several distinct peaks in the frequency domain. What are the frequencies of the peaks in Hz?
% Record your answers in the vector freqPeaks
% e.g., freqPeaks = [2 5 10]
freqPeaks = [100 120 150];
%%
%[text] **6.** Create an audio signal of the form
%[text]{"align":"center"} $x(t) = \\sum\_{n=1}^3 b\_n \\sin\\left(2 \\pi f\_n t\\right)${"altText":"x(t) = \sum\_{n=1}^3 b\_n \sin\left(2 \pi f\_n t\right)"}
%[text] with only three terms: the frequencies of the peaks you identified in the audio signal in **5**. Set the coefficient of the highest frequency to 0.1 and the coefficient of the two lower frequencies to 0.2.
%[text] Define your signal `x` in terms of the variable `t`. 
t = 0:1/Fs:3; % Use this variable
% Write your code here
x = 0.1*sin(2*pi*150*t) + 0.2*sin(2*pi*120*t) + 0.2*sin(2*pi*100*t);
%%
%[text] **7.** Plot the signal $x${"altText":"x"} against $t${"altText":"t"}. 
plot(t,x)
xlabel("t (s)")
ylabel("x")
%%
%[text] **8.** Play the signal $x${"altText":"x"} as an audio sound using the [`sound`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/sound.html) function.
sound(x,Fs)
%%
%[text] **9.** Compute the Fourier coefficients of `x` by uncommenting the line below. Then, compute the magnitude of the coefficients. Store the value in `Ax`.
[freq,a,b] = computeFourierCoef(x,Fs);
Ax = sqrt(a.^2 + b.^2);
%%
%[text] **10.** Plot the magnitude vector `A` against the frequency vector `freq`, which has units of Hz. Set the horizontal axis limits to `[0 200]` Hz.
plot(freq,Ax)
xlim([0 200])
xlabel("Frequency (Hz)")
ylabel("Magnitude")
%%
%[text] **11.** Compare original signal $y${"altText":"y"} to the signal $x${"altText":"x"} you created using only three frequencies. 
%[text] - What similarity(ies) do you notice between the two signals? 
%[text] - What is the main difference between them? \
% Record your answers below in the quotes.
similarity = "Similar frequency content."
difference = "No amplitude modulation in x(t)."
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](file:../../MainMenu.m)
%%
%[text] ## Help us Improve
%[text] [Share your feedback](<matlab: ShareFeedback>) to help us improve this module.
%%
function [freq,a,b] = computeFourierCoef(y,Fs)
% This helper function computes the Fourier coefficients
% of a signal y(t) with sampling frequency Fs

    % FFT
    Y = fft(y);

    % Fourier series coefficients from the FFT
    N = numel(y);
    Nterms = round(N/2);
    YHalf = Y(2:Nterms);
    a0 = real(Y(1))/N;
    a = [a0,2/N*real(YHalf)];
    b = [0,-2/N*imag(YHalf)];

    % The frequencies of the coefficients
    freq = Fs/N*(0:(Nterms-1))';
end


